Adrenal tumors form in one or both adrenal glands, which sit atop the kidneys and are part of the endocrine system. Most adrenal tumors are benign; adrenal cancer (adrenocortical carcinoma, ACC) is rare, occurring most often in children ages 5 and under and adults in their 40s and 50s.

Symptoms: About half of adrenal cancer patients have symptoms caused by hormones from the tumor; others have symptoms only if the tumor grows large enough to press on nearby organs. Reported symptoms include nausea, vomiting, abdominal bloating, back pain, fever, loss of appetite, unintended weight loss, enlarged breast tissue, shrinking testicles, and pain when touched. Patients should tell their doctor about any pain or lumps, including enlarged lymph nodes in the neck, underarm, or groin.

Risk factors: There are no known causes for most adrenal tumors, but hereditary (genetic) conditions passed from biological parent to child increase risk and account for a small portion of cases. These include Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome; Carney complex; familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP); hereditary paraganglioma-pheochromocytoma (PGL/PCC) syndrome; Li-Fraumeni syndrome; Lynch syndrome;
